# Line endings, declared once so no editor and no tool can decide them per file.
#
# Why this file exists. The repository was predominantly LF with five CRLF files and no policy, on a
# Windows machine. Any tool that writes CRLF into an LF file produces a diff that rewrites every line,
# which hides the actual change from review. That happened five times during the audit of 2026-08-30
# and each occurrence had to be found and repaired by hand. Declaring the policy here moves the fix
# from vigilance to mechanism: git normalises on staging, so the diff shows the change and nothing else.
#
# Recorded as the resolution of D-DEV-02 in .agent/DECISIONS.md.

# Everything git detects as text is stored LF in the repository and checked out LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell is the exception. Windows PowerShell 5.1 tolerates LF, but signed scripts and here-strings
# are sensitive to it and the bootstrap script is the one file an operator runs before anything works.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary, so git never inspects or converts them. Visual baselines in particular must be byte exact:
# a single converted byte turns the whole suite red for a reason unrelated to the interface.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.bundle binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary
*.m4a binary

# Generated, and reviewed as a whole rather than line by line.
uv.lock linguist-generated=true
package-lock.json linguist-generated=true
packages/shared-types/src/generated.ts linguist-generated=true
